module lib/hf/integrals
  The integrals Hartree-Fock needs, each stage behind one call that can run
  several independent implementations and require them to agree.

  A Hartree-Fock energy needs four kinds of numbers from the basis set: the
  contracted coefficients, the one-electron matrices S (overlap), T (kinetic
  energy) and V (nuclear attraction), the electron repulsion integrals (ERIs)
  `(ij|kl)`, and, for a spherical basis, the same numbers moved from Cartesian
  to spherical functions. Each stage has a registry: an array of functions
  that compute the same thing by methods that share no algebra, production
  member first. A contract def runs the members a mask selects through
  lib/hf/sweep, takes their elementwise median as the answer, and fails when
  any member is farther from it than the stage's tolerance. With
  `release_mode()`, or a mask that selects one member, only that member runs.

int_contract_closed  @cdc86fb3
  why   The registry's production member: the normalization lib/hf/basis
        already computes, wrapped to the `(atoms, shells, prims)` signature
        every contraction member has.
  atoms   the atoms table; not read, it is there for the signature
  shells  the shells table, one row per shell
  prims   the primitives table: each primitive's exponent and coefficient

int_prim_overlaps  @c8473cb1
  why   The numeric contraction needs the overlaps of one shell's primitives
        from an integral code. Making each primitive a one-primitive shell at
        the origin lets an ordinary overlap member compute them.
  shells  the shells table
  prims   the primitives table
  s       the shell whose primitives are wanted
  which   which overlap code computes them: `0` md1_s, anything else os_overlap
  l       the shell's angular momentum
  np      how many primitives the shell has
  p0      the row of its first primitive in prims
  nc      how many Cartesian components a shell of this l has
  at      a one-atom table at the origin
  sh      the temporary shells table: one shell per primitive, all on that atom
  pr      the temporary primitives table: the shell's exponents, coefficient 1
  S       the overlap matrix of every component of the temporary shells

  = sh#2   each primitive becomes its own shell, with the angular momentum of the one it came from
  = pr#2   the exponent is copied; the coefficient is set to 1 so the overlap is that of the normalized primitive
  = S#2    member `0` computes the overlaps by McMurchie-Davidson
  = S#3    any other `which` uses Obara-Saika; the result keeps entry `[p nc, q nc]`, the first component of each shell, since every component gives the same radial overlap

int_contract_by  @f0fbc4fd
  why   It is the numeric form of the normalization: the self-overlap of the
        contracted shell is taken from an integral code, not the closed
        formula, so the two share only the radial factor.
  shells  the shells table
  prims   the primitives table
  which   the overlap member to use, as in int_prim_overlaps
  cn      the result: one normalized contraction coefficient per primitive
  c       the current shell's coefficients
  ss      the self-overlap of the contracted shell with the unscaled coefficients
  scale   the factor that makes that self-overlap 1

bas_contract_numeric  @64df4039
  atoms   the atoms table; not read
  shells  the shells table
  prims   the primitives table

bas_contract_numeric_os  @da4a7240
  atoms   the atoms table; not read
  shells  the shells table
  prims   the primitives table

int_contract_methods  @023b5a22
  what  The contraction registry: closed form first, then the two numeric
        members, one taking the overlaps from McMurchie-Davidson and one from
        Obara-Saika.
  why   Three routes to the same coefficients that share only the radial
        factor, so a slip in either integral code or in the formula shows up
        as a dissent [test "the numeric contraction agrees with the closed form on water 6-31G* and random shells"].

int_overlap_methods  @88d693b8
  what  The overlap registry: McMurchie-Davidson (`md1_s`, production),
        Obara-Saika (`os_overlap`) and Gauss-Hermite quadrature (`gh_overlap`).
  why   Two recurrences and a quadrature that share no code. lib/hf/hf runs
        the production member alone unless the options ask for a cross-check
        [test "S, T and V from every member match PySCF on water 6-31G*"].

int_kinetic_methods  @7d03c24a
  what  The kinetic-energy registry: `md1_t` (production), `os_kinetic` and
        `gh_kinetic`, from the same three families as the overlap.

int_nuclear_methods  @2b41cee9
  what  The nuclear-attraction registry: `md1_v` (production) and `os_nuclear`.
  why   It has no quadrature member, because the Coulomb potential of a
        nucleus is singular and Gauss-Hermite points integrate only smooth
        factors.

int_eri_methods  @da5f6456
  what  The electron-repulsion registry: McMurchie-Davidson on the kernels
        lib/cas generated (`mdg_screened`, production), the general
        McMurchie-Davidson code with Schwarz screening (`mderi_screened`) and
        without (`mderi_full`), Head-Gordon-Pople (`hgp_eri`) and Rys
        quadrature (`rys_eri`). Each returns the packed-8 vector of
        lib/hf/eri_store.
  why   The generated member is held to the general code it was generated
        from; HGP and Rys share no recurrence with MD except the Boys
        function, and all of them agree with each other and with PySCF
        [test "all five ERI members agree on water STO-3G and match PySCF's full set"].
        Rys costs far more than the others, so the ERI contract takes a mask
        and the default runs generated MD and HGP (`INT_ERI_PAIR`).

int_c2s_closed  @dfb604f1
  why   The sweep driver for this stage passes `(nmax, x)`; this wrapper gives
        `c2s_closed` that signature, and `x` is not read.
  l   the angular momentum of the shell
  x   not read

int_c2s_general  @b985b8de
  what  Member `1` of the Cartesian-to-spherical registry: `c2s_general(l)`,
        the general formula for `M_l`, in the `(nmax, x)` signature.
  l   the angular momentum of the shell
  x   not read

int_c2s_methods  @52c04c9e
  what  The Cartesian-to-spherical registry: the closed tables for small l
        (production) and the general formula.

int_tr2_methods  @637dd663
  what  The registry that moves a one-electron matrix to spherical functions,
        `M^T X M`: shell block by shell block (`tr2_blocks`, production), or
        as two dense matrix products (`tr2_dense`).

int_tr2_back_methods  @fd736f23
  what  The registry that moves a spherical density back to Cartesian
        functions, `M D M^T`: block by block (`tr2_back_blocks`) or dense
        (`tr2_back_dense`).
  why   Only int_cart2 uses it, and no program calls int_cart2; the direct
        J/K member does its own back-transform inline.

int_tr4_methods  @8efa4dd2
  what  The registry that moves packed ERIs from Cartesian to spherical
        functions: shell block by shell block (`tr4_blocks`, production), or
        one index at a time on the unpacked tensor (`tr4_quarter`).
  why   `tr4_quarter` unpacks to `[n, n, n, n]`, so int_tr4_mask lets it run
        only for small bases.

int_jk_methods  @95075073
  why   lib/hf/jk holds the in-core members and cannot use this module, so
        the registry that adds the two direct members lives here. `opts[OPT_JK]`
        picks the member the SCF runs.

int_mask_has  @43e741e5
  why   A mask lets a caller choose which members of a registry run: bit k
        set means member k runs. The expensive Rys member can then be left
        out by default [test "each registry holds the members the page lists"].
  mask  the member mask; `0` selects every member
  k     the member's index in the registry

int_mask_count  @f6fa7b4e
  why   The drivers need to know whether more than one member runs, and
        which one runs first, before they build the comparison table.
  mask   the member mask
  n      how many members the registry has
  first  the first selected member, `-1` if none
  c      how many members are selected so far
  m      the member being tested

int_run3  @0598b82e
  why   The one place the registry is run and compared for the three-array
        signature; int_sweep3 and int_check3 differ only in what they do
        with the verdict. The median, not member 0's result, is returned, so a
        bad production member is blamed instead of the members that disagree
        with it [test "the masked driver runs the selected members and blames by registry index"].
  ms       the registry
  mask     which members run
  x1       the first argument every member receives
  x2       the second
  x3       the third
  N        how many numbers a member returns
  rep      the report: blamed member, flat index, worst deviation, members run
  release  `1` to run only the first selected member
  cnt      how many members are selected
  f        the first selected member's registry index
  ix       the flat indices `0 .. N-1`, to read any shape as a vector
  T        `[cnt, N]`: one row per member run, its result flattened
  who      the registry index of each row's member
  out      the first member's result; the consensus is written into it at the end
  r        the next free row of T
  cur      the result of the member being run
  C        the elementwise median of the rows

  = rep     one member runs alone: the report says nothing was compared and names that member
  = out     each member gets its own copies of the arguments (`x1 + 0.0`), so one that writes into them cannot change what the next one sees
  = rep#2   the blamed row of the table is turned back into the member's index in the registry

int_sweep3  @324efe4a
  why   Every integral stage of lib/hf/hf goes through here: in debug runs a
        dissent stops the program at the stage where it happened.
  ms       the registry
  mask     which members run
  x1       the first argument every member receives
  x2       the second
  x3       the third
  N        how many numbers a member returns
  tol      the largest relative deviation from the median allowed
  rep      the report, filled by int_run3
  release  `1` when the program runs in release mode
  R        the consensus

int_check3  @d41e38b1
  why   Tests plant a bad member and need the verdict as a value, not a failed
        assertion [test "the masked driver runs the selected members and blames by registry index"].
  ms    the registry
  mask  which members run
  x1    the first argument every member receives
  x2    the second
  x3    the third
  N     how many numbers a member returns
  tol   the largest relative deviation allowed
  rep   the report
  out   receives the consensus
  R     the consensus from int_run3
  ix    the flat indices `0 .. N-1`

int_run5  @15a0cd11
  why   A Viv def is typed by the one registry signature it is called with, so
        the five-argument J/K members need a driver of their own; it is
        int_run3 with two more arguments.
  ms       the J/K registry
  mask     which members run
  x1       the packed ERIs
  x2       the basis blob, for the direct member
  x3       the basis blob again
  x4       the densities
  x5       the run's options
  N        how many numbers a member returns
  rep      the report
  release  `1` to run only the first selected member
  cnt      how many members are selected
  f        the first selected member's registry index
  ix       the flat indices `0 .. N-1`
  T        one row per member run, its result flattened
  who      the registry index of each row's member
  out      the first member's result, then the consensus
  r        the next free row of T
  cur      the result of the member being run
  C        the elementwise median of the rows

